---
import HardwareNote from "../../layouts/HardwareNote.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/specs";
import HwSection from "../../components/HwSection.astro";
import HwFigure from "../../components/HwFigure.astro";
import HwNote from "../../components/HwNote.astro";

// The three lines that were on the console. Nothing else was.
const symptom = `[  OK  ] Finished Create Volatile Files and Directories in the Real Root.
<em>You are in emergency mode.</em> After logging in, type "journalctl -xb" to view
system logs, "systemctl reboot" to reboot, or "exit" to continue bootup.

<em>Cannot open access to console, the root account is locked.</em>
See sulogin(8) man page for more details.`;

// nixos/modules/system/boot/systemd/initrd.nix, abridged to the branch that
// fired. <b> is the line that ends the boot.
const unit = `<i># initrd-find-nixos-closure.service</i>
<i># RequiresMountsFor = "/sysroot/nix/store"</i>
<i># requiredBy        = [ "initrd.target" ]</i>

closure=
for o in $(&lt; /proc/cmdline); do
    case $o in
        init=*) … closure="\${initParam[1]}" ;;
    esac
done

if [ -z "\${closure:-}" ]; then
  echo 'No init= parameter on the kernel command line' &gt;&amp;2
  <b>exit 1</b>
fi`;

// board/canaan/common/k230_img.c, verbatim from line 110.
const vendor = `<i>// u-boot/board/canaan/common/k230_img.c:110</i>
char *board_fdt_chosen_bootargs(void){
    char *bootargs = <b>env_get("bootargs")</b>;
    if(NULL == bootargs) {
        …
        bootargs = <em>"root=/dev/mmcblk1p2 loglevel=8 rw rootdelay=4 \\
                    rootfstype=ext4 console=ttyS0,115200  earlycon=sbi"</em>;
    }
    printf("g_bootmod = %d, bootargs=%s\\n", g_bootmod, bootargs);
    return bootargs;
}`;

// The environment as tools/gen-stage1.sh now writes it. <b> is what the sed
// added; the rest is the vendor's own default.env.
const blinux = `blinux = k230_set_dtb &&
         <b>ext4load mmc \${mmc_boot_dev_num}:1 0x7000000 /bootargs.txt &&</b>
         <b>env import -t 0x7000000 \${filesize} &&</b>
         ext4load mmc \${mmc_boot_dev_num}:1 <mark>0x8000000</mark> /fw_jump_add_uboot_head.bin &&
         ext4load mmc \${mmc_boot_dev_num}:1 0x200000  /\${k} &&
         ext4load mmc \${mmc_boot_dev_num}:1 <mark>0x8400000</mark> /\${dtb} &&
         <b>ext4load mmc \${mmc_boot_dev_num}:1 0x9000000 /initrd.uimg &&</b>
         bootm <mark>0x8000000 0x9000000 0x8400000</mark>

<i>mmc_boot_dev_num = 1     k = Image     dtb = force.dtb</i>`;
---

<HardwareNote
  slug="boot-chain"
  title="The command line the bootloader threw away"
  eyebrow="LILYGO T-Display-K230 · hardware note · observed"
  dek="The device tree carried a correct kernel command line. U&#8209;Boot replaced it with a vendor string that had no <code>init=</code> in it, NixOS stage&nbsp;1 gave up, and the board stopped in emergency mode with the root account locked. One <code>if</code> in <code>k230_img.c</code> explains all of it."
  description="Why NixOS stage 1 dropped to emergency mode on the T-Display-K230: U-Boot's board_fdt_chosen_bootargs() overwrites /chosen/bootargs unless the environment defines bootargs, and the fix that beats it."
  source="docs/evidence/stage1-emergency-mode.md"
  meta={[
    { label: "Symptom", value: "emergency mode<br>root locked, no shell offered" },
    { label: "Failing unit", value: "initrd-find-nixos-closure<br>.service, exit 1" },
    { label: "Cause", value: "board/canaan/common<br>/k230_img.c:110" },
    { label: "Fix", value: "bootargs.txt on the boot<br>partition + <code>env import</code>" },
    { label: "Confirmed", value: "2026-09-20, /proc/cmdline<br>on the booted board" },
  ]}
>
  <HwSection kind="the symptom" title="Three lines, and two of them are good news">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      This is everything the console said. It looks like a catastrophe and it is
      very nearly a success &mdash; which is the useful thing about reading it
      carefully rather than reaching for the card reader.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={symptom} />

    <div class="hw-q">
      <div>
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The root filesystem <em>is</em> mounted</h3>
          <p>
            &ldquo;in the Real Root&rdquo; is the <code>/sysroot</code> variant of
            the tmpfiles unit. It cannot possibly run before
            <code>sysroot.mount</code> has succeeded. So the card, the partition
            table, the ext4 filesystem and the MMC driver are all fine.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The failure is the very next unit</h3>
          <p>
            Something ordered immediately after that tmpfiles unit failed, and
            failed fatally to <code>initrd.target</code>. That is a short list.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The locked root account is why it looks worse than it is</h3>
          <p>
            Emergency mode normally drops you into a shell. NixOS leaves root
            locked, so <code>sulogin</code> has nothing to offer and the boot simply
            stops. That is a NixOS default meeting a bad day, not a second fault.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the unit" title="initrd-find-nixos-closure.service, and its one demand">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      From <code>nixos/modules/system/boot/systemd/initrd.nix</code>. It reads
      <code>/proc/cmdline</code>, looks for <code>init=</code>, and if it does not
      find one it refuses to continue &mdash; because without it, stage 1 does not
      know which of the closures on the disk it is supposed to be booting.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={unit} />

    <p class="hw-p">
      <code>RequiresMountsFor = "/sysroot/nix/store"</code> is what orders it
      immediately after the sysroot tmpfiles unit, and
      <code>requiredBy = [ "initrd.target" ]</code> is what turns its
      <code>exit 1</code> into emergency mode. The entire failure therefore reduces
      to one sentence: <strong>there was no <code>init=</code> on the kernel command
      line.</strong>
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the cause" title="U-Boot honours /chosen/bootargs only when it feels like it">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      The SD image derivation writes the command line into the device tree with
      <code>fdtput -t s … /chosen bootargs</code>, and that step is genuinely
      required &mdash; an earlier boot with no <code>/chosen/bootargs</code> at all
      was silent on both UARTs. It is also, on its own, useless. Vendored U-Boot
      rewrites the property on its way past.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={vendor} />

    <p class="hw-p">
      The vendor environment never defines <code>bootargs</code>, so every boot took
      the second branch. The <code>g_bootmod = 3</code> line printed on every one of
      those boots is line 126 of this same function &mdash; it had been sitting in
      the log the whole time, naming the function that was doing it.
    </p>

    <HwFigure
      caption="<strong>The decision, and the two boots either side of it.</strong> Note what the left branch costs: whatever <code>fdtput</code> wrote into <code>/chosen/bootargs</code> is discarded before the kernel ever sees the tree. The device tree is not where the kernel command line lives on this board."
    >
