    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="The coincidence that made it look like progress">
      <p>
        The failing boots got as far as a mounted root filesystem, which read as
        evidence that our storage configuration was correct. It was not evidence of
        that at all. The vendor fallback string happens to carry
        <code>root=/dev/mmcblk1p2</code>, and our partition layout happens to match
        it. The root mount that appeared to work was the bootloader&rsquo;s doing.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the shape of bug worth naming: a wrong configuration that is rescued
        by a coincidence produces a <em>partial</em> success, and a partial success
        is much harder to read than a clean failure.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the fix" title="Define the variable, and win the test outright">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      <code>env_get("bootargs")</code> is checked first, so anything that defines it
      wins. The interesting part is <em>where</em> the value is allowed to live: the
      command line names the NixOS closure and therefore changes on every rebuild,
      while the stage 1 environment image must not, because it is vendored firmware
      data with a recorded hash.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={blinux} />

    <div class="hw-q">
      <div>
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The value lives on the boot partition, not in the image</h3>
          <p>
            <code>nix/sd-image.nix</code> writes <code>bootargs.txt</code> from
            <code>config.boot.kernelParams</code> plus <code>init=</code> &mdash;
            the same string it puts in the DTB, so the two cannot drift apart.
            <code>env.env</code> stays closure-independent.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <div>
          <h3><code>env import</code> is available because the build enables it</h3>
          <p>
            <code>CONFIG_CMD_IMPORTENV=y</code> in this U&#8209;Boot build.
            <code>ext4load</code> sets the <code>filesize</code> variable as a side
            effect (<code>fs/fs.c:743</code>), which the very next command consumes
            &mdash; the two lines only work as a pair, and in that order.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>0x7000000 is chosen, not arbitrary</h3>
          <p>
            Above the ~60&nbsp;MB kernel that lands at <code>0x200000</code> and
            below <code>fw_jump</code> at <code>0x8000000</code>. The highlighted
            addresses in the listing are the same problem solved once before: the
            vendor layout assumes a kernel under 32&nbsp;MiB, ours is not, and the
            device tree and OpenSBI payload had to move above it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <HwNote kind="grounded" label="Confirmed end to end">
      <p>
        <code>cat /proc/cmdline</code> on the booted board returns exactly the
        string <code>bootargs.txt</code> carries, <code>init=</code> included. The
        transcript is on the <a href={url("hardware/first-boot/")}>first-boot
        note</a>.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="What the fix did not settle, and what to suspect next">
      <p>
        <code>root=fstab</code> resolved correctly on the first boot after the fix,
        which closes a risk that had been open since the vendor fallback started
        quietly supplying an explicit <code>root=</code>. One success is not a
        pattern. If <code>sysroot</code> ever fails to mount on a later boot, the
        <code>NIXOS_SD</code> label path is the thing to suspect, not this.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="corrections" title="Three things this project believed that the board disproved">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Written from the QEMU side before the board had ever run our image, repeated
      in this repository, and wrong. Each one cost a boot cycle, which is why they
      are named here rather than quietly edited out.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-contract">
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-head">
        <span>was believed</span>
        <span>is actually</span>
        <span>why it mattered</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-hard">
        <div class="a">U-Boot reads extlinux</div>
        <div class="b">nothing on this board parses extlinux.conf</div>
        <div class="c">
          Vendored U-Boot runs <code>blinux</code>, an environment variable holding
          a chain of <code>ext4load</code> commands that fetch each file by literal
          filename, ending in <code>bootm</code>. There is no generation menu and no
          <code>APPEND</code> line to put <code>init=</code> on, which is why
          <code>nix/hardware.nix</code> sets
          <code>boot.loader.generic-extlinux-compatible.enable = false</code>.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-hard">
        <div class="a">OpenSBI is v0.9</div>
        <div class="b">v1.4, on the path that boots Linux</div>
        <div class="c">
          v0.9 is what the shipped RT&#8209;Smart firmware prints, and that log is
          real. It is not our chain. We boot through
          <code>opensbi-1.4-overlay</code> &mdash; OpenSBI 1.4 plus Canaan&rsquo;s
          T&#8209;Head overlay, <code>FW_TEXT_START=0</code> &mdash; and the version
          matters because the overlay carries
          <code>THEAD_QUIRK_DISABLE_MAEE</code>.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-hard">
        <div class="a">the DTB carries the kernel command line</div>
        <div class="b">the U-Boot environment does</div>
        <div class="c">
          This is the sharpest difference from QEMU, where <code>-append</code> is
          simply honoured. On the board the command line is a property of the
          environment image and the boot partition. Writing it into the device tree
          is still necessary, and is not sufficient.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <div class="hw-sources">
    <p>
      Analysis: docs/evidence/stage1-emergency-mode.md. Console evidence:
      docs/evidence/hardware-boot.txt for the failure,
      docs/evidence/hardware-userspace.md for /proc/cmdline after the fix. The
      vendor environment as shipped is in docs/evidence/uboot-env.txt; the
      differences from QEMU are tabulated in docs/evidence/boot-path-differences.md.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vendor source: u-boot/board/canaan/common/k230_img.c line 110 (the function)
      and line 126 (the g_bootmod printf). NixOS source:
      nixos/modules/system/boot/systemd/initrd.nix. Our side:
      nix/sd-image.nix, nix/hardware.nix, tools/gen-stage1.sh and
      firmware/stage1/PROVENANCE.txt, which records every byte of stage 1 that this
      project generates rather than vendors.
    </p>
    <p>
      The U-Boot and SPL binaries are untouched. Only the environment image &mdash;
      plain data produced with <code>mkenvimage</code> from a text file &mdash; is
      ours, which is what <code>image/boot-chain</code> requires.
    </p>
  </div>
</HardwareNote>
